   Synthetic data is useful for regression, but not calibration.

7. End with the business message.

   The tool reduces wasted assessment effort and exposes storage capability gaps before the program commits to a pilot or platform scope.

## Defensible Core-Technology Elements

The repo has several defensible technical assets:

1. Evidence graph

   Observations, inferences, warnings, and missing facts are explicitly modeled.

2. Storage behavior fingerprint

   The tool translates VM evidence into structured behavior dimensions such as shared namespace dependency, writer topology, raw block likelihood, latency sensitivity, expansion need, and evidence quality.

3. Formal requirement extraction

   The engine converts behavior into hard requirements, soft preferences, and unresolved dimensions.

4. Constraint solver

   Storage classes are evaluated against explicit constraints, with hard failures, soft warnings, and counterfactuals.

5. Evidence questions

   REVIEW results produce next-step questions that can change the decision.

6. Quality gates

   The workflow distinguishes technical output from business-review or release-ready evidence.

7. Calibration workflow

   The project has a path from synthetic regression cases to expert-consensus and outcome-linked evidence.

8. Local-only privacy posture

   No network calls, no credentials, no cluster access, and redacted bundles make the tool distributable.

9. Portfolio aggregation

   A single-workload engine can roll up into capability demand without becoming a migration wave planner.

10. Data contracts and schemas

   Versioned artifact contracts support repeatability, auditability, and partner handoff.

## Realistic ROI Categories

The strongest ROI is operational and risk-adjusted:

1. Avoided failed pilots

   Catching RWX, raw block, capacity, or latency blockers before a pilot saves platform, app, DBA, and storage team time.

2. Faster qualification

   PASS cases can move forward with less expert escalation. REVIEW cases get targeted questions. FAIL cases expose profile gaps.

3. Better platform scope

   Capability demand helps platform teams avoid underbuilding storage options for the real estate.

4. Reduced expert toil

   Storage architects and DBAs spend time on the small set of high-risk cases rather than every VM.

5. Lower rework risk

   Decisions are reason-coded and tied to storage profile constraints, so misunderstandings are caught earlier.

6. Better partner credibility

   A partner can show evidence, caveats, and next questions instead of hand-wavy modernization claims.

7. Governance confidence

   Quality gates, provenance, and calibration boundaries help prevent unsupported claims from reaching leadership.

8. Safer handling of sensitive estates

   Redacted local bundles reduce friction when collecting early evidence.

## Remaining Limitations And Caveats

These caveats should remain visible in every serious demo:

1. A short `iostat` sample is not a benchmark.

   It can signal risk but cannot prove target-platform performance.

2. Process names are hints.

   They do not prove full application topology or every dependency.

3. Root-only storage visibility lowers confidence.

   Durable data may be hidden under root paths unless owners declare application data paths.

4. Shared filesystem detection does not explain why sharing exists.

   It may be required, incidental, read-only, operational, or architectural.

5. Block-backed filesystem is not raw block.

   The tool correctly separates `Filesystem` and `Block` volume modes.

6. Capacity headroom is heuristic.

   Growth rates and retention behavior require owner evidence.

7. The tool does not connect to a cluster.

   It evaluates a supplied profile, not live cluster behavior.

8. The tool does not prove application correctness.

   Storage fit is one dimension of readiness.

9. Synthetic generated data is not real-world validation.

   The repo correctly excludes synthetic cases from calibration readiness.

10. Business impact requires measured records.

   Generated impact data remains simulated-only or evidence-missing unless real records are supplied.

11. Multi-VM and distributed app relationships are outside the one-VM MVP.

   Shared dependencies, replication, failover, and quorum behavior need separate owner evidence.

12. Identity, permissions, and security contexts are not fully modeled.

   UID/GID, SELinux, fsGroup, and application-level permissions remain unresolved dimensions.

## Recommended Next Product Moves

To turn this into more defensible core technology with real business impact:

1. Build a real-case acquisition program.

   Collect redacted, owner-enriched cases from actual assessments.

2. Require expert review and adjudication.

   Each serious case should have at least two reviewers or an adjudicated label.

3. Link outcomes.

   Track whether the workload onboarded successfully, was blocked by storage, required storage rework, or failed a storage assumption.

4. Improve workload-family policy packs.

   Extend relational database, file server, search/logging, queue/streaming, and generic app packs with field-tested thresholds.

5. Expand evidence questions into owner worksheets.

   Convert questions into a concise app-owner handoff form.

6. Add richer profile quality checks.

   Detect unrealistic max sizes, missing expansion data, missing snapshot policy, and vague performance tiers.

7. Add multi-VM relationship metadata.

   Keep the one-VM engine, but allow portfolio cases to declare shared clusters, replication groups, and failover relationships.

8. Add performance validation handoff.

   Generate a deeper validation plan when latency is high, without running benchmarks automatically.

9. Improve calibration reports.

   Report false PASS rate, false FAIL rate, REVIEW usefulness, expert disagreement rate, and policy-pack readiness by family.

10. Keep the local-only and vendor-neutral boundary.

   This is part of the product's trust model.

## Bottom Line

The top business story is:

> `stateful-storage-fit` helps platform, storage, database, and application teams find out early whether a VM-based stateful workload plausibly fits the target persistent storage options, what capability gaps exist, and what evidence is needed next.

That has real business value because stateful storage mismatch is one of the practical ways modernization programs burn time: a workload looks easy until RWX, raw block, capacity, latency, topology, or ownership details appear too late. This repo turns those risks into deterministic, reviewable, local evidence.

